5 mg/dL)", "Unintentional weight loss"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Gastrointestinal / Nutritional Toxicity", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.5991739257979186, "posterior": 0.3289495184356004, "danger": 0.5, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Hypercalcemia with low urine sodium is consistent with milk-alkali syndrome (antacid overuse) but not definitive due to non-specific lab findings."], "evidence_against": ["Subacute constitutional symptoms are less typical of antacid overuse and provide moderate support for malignancy as an underlying cause."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.4, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Endocrine / Parathyroid Disorder", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.09390069572566302, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Normal phosphorus and no bone pain provide weak support for primary hyperparathyroidism, but elevated alkaline phosphatase shifts probability toward malignancy."], "evidence_against": ["Elevated alkaline phosphatase strongly supports malignancy-related hypercalcemia and weakly argues against primary hyperparathyroidism."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B2.1", "B2.2"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Malignancy-Related Hypercalcemia", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.028169913438446804, "posterior": 0.0010499508406714797, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "expla